commit 315656759859fc697d9336d28636bc6c96ca950f
Author: María <mariagrandury@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Sep 22 02:13:36 2026 +0200

    evals: fix the rfgm validator's per-language item loss
    
    The per-choice script check added with the online mode rejected 30.9 % of
    Japanese INCLUDE items against 0-2 % in the other seven languages: it took
    the majority Unicode script per choice, and Japanese mixes kanji and kana
    within a sentence, so the majority flipped for reasons unrelated to drift.
    Uneven item loss across languages is the one bias a per-language comparison
    cannot absorb.
    
    script_of now folds kana, katakana, hangul and han into one class, and the
    per-choice check applies only to options the source wrote in the item's own
    script -- an option that was already off-script (a bare "a, b", a Latin
    formula) is not evidence of drift. Real drift is still caught: a kanji to
    romaji rewrite, and the Greek item whose numerals came back in Latin, both
    still fail.
    
    self_ref also catches bare label lists ("a, b", "b, c, d", "II"), which the
    a) regex missed. Rejects now record what the model produced, so one can be
    diagnosed or re-judged without paying for the call again, and
    online --retry-rejects re-runs a previous pass's failures.
    
    Adds --L (keep only the tasks an L-cell's languages train, 124 of 185 at
    L50; the ladder scores no others) and narrows report by --family/--tasks.
    
    Verified on the real sets: Japanese recovered 250 of 257 retried items,
    INCLUDE rejects fell 581 -> 158 of 22,087 (0.72 %) and "a choice changed
    script" 318 -> 7. The first complete rfgm set is reported in
    rfgm_include_items.md: 21,929 items, and the answer-shape leak is flat at
    23.3 % rewritten against 23.2 % original, both under the 25 % chance line,
    with the gold-distractor gap unchanged at 0.65 -> 0.66 tokens.
    check_rules: 0 findings.

